# Verification Policy

Code generation is never completion.

## Mandatory verification matrix

Apply every matching row:

| Change type | Required verification |
| --- | --- |
| Pure business/domain logic | unit tests |
| Database/model/migration | unit + migration/integration test |
| HTTP/API behavior | unit + integration + contract/schema checks |
| Cross-service contract | contract + integration + compatibility check |
| Frontend component | component/unit where available + accessibility + responsive + visual-state checks |
| Major UI surface | desktop/laptop/tablet/mobile evidence + loading/empty/error/state coverage + console check |
| User-visible workflow | E2E or equivalent end-to-end evidence |
| Motion/animation | reduced-motion + interaction-blocking + state-clarity checks |
| Terminal UI/mock | deterministic command/state tests + keyboard/resize/accessibility checks |
| Lab session lifecycle | start + health + stop/expire + deterministic cleanup |
| Runner/runtime provider | lifecycle + resource limit + failure/recovery tests |
| Network/security boundary | positive path + negative tests proving forbidden access fails |
| Authorization/tenant change | positive role test + cross-role + cross-tenant negative tests |
| Supply-chain/lab import | license metadata + pin/digest + schema + build/test/scan evidence |
| Policy/ruleset change | policy CI + hook tests + version consistency |

For UI work, `docs/ui/README.md` and rules 13–17 are mandatory verification inputs. Pytest alone is not sufficient evidence for meaningful visual changes.

## Security order

For security-sensitive changes:
1. implementation owner finishes candidate change,
2. quanta-security reviews,
3. owner fixes findings,
4. quanta-verifier independently verifies.

## Completion output

A task is complete only when evidence states:
- what was tested,
- viewport/state coverage where applicable,
- what passed,
- what remains unverified, if anything.

If required verification cannot run, report BLOCKED rather than DONE.
